{"result": {"response": {"id": "chatcmpl-EIXGN0w8AH3uvegZFYSWOCWdLMM9I", "choices": [{"finish_reason": "stop", "index": 0, "logprobs": null, "message": {"content": "{\"title\":\"Process Injection and Image-Evasion Techniques\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on Windows process-injection and executable-image evasion techniques, including Process Hollowing, Process Herpaderping, Process Ghosting, Process Doppelgänging, Image Spoofing, and Module Stomping. These techniques are connected through their use of manipulated process memory, deceptive executable content, file-system behavior, or legitimate-looking process images to conceal malicious execution. Supporting technical entities include CreateProcess, WriteProcessMemory(), suspended processes, process resumption, Transactional NTFS, and Notepad.exe as a benign-looking target. The community also includes detection approaches—process memory/file comparison and telemetry-based manipulation detection—and references to Black Hills Information Security, F-Secure, and DarkRelay as sources associated with related research. The evidence describes high-impact defense-evasion capabilities, but does not establish use by a particular actor, victimization, legal noncompliance, or confirmed malicious activity in a specific environment.\",\"findings\":[{\"summary\":\"Process evasion is the community’s central capability\",\"explanation\":\"The strongest common theme is evasion of endpoint security inspection. Process Herpaderping replaces malicious executable content with benign content before scanners can inspect it, while Process Ghosting executes malicious content from a pending-deletion file, complicating file-based inspection [Data: Entities (477, 478); Relationships (587, 588)]. Image Spoofing similarly makes malicious code or process memory appear associated with a legitimate executable, helping conceal activity from security controls and forensic analysis [Data: Entities (473); Relationships (579, 600)].\\n\\nTogether, these entities describe a capability cluster focused on presenting benign artifacts while malicious code is executing. The evidence supports assessing this as a technically significant defense-evasion community, but it does not identify an operator or confirm deployment in a real incident.\"},{\"summary\":\"Process Hollowing links process creation, memory replacement, and execution\",\"explanation\":\"Process Hollowing is connected to a sequence in which a legitimate process—specifically Notepad.exe in the supplied example—is created in a suspended state, its original contents are overwritten, and execution is later resumed [Data: Entities (475, 472, 488, 490, 489); Relationships (583, 602, 604, 606, 607)]. CreateProcess is identified as the process-creation API used to launch the initial target process, while WriteProcessMemory() is identified as the API used to overwrite the suspended process’s memory [Data: Entities (479, 400); Relationships (605)].\\n\\nThis creates a coherent technical chain from process creation to memory manipulation and process resumption. The data indicates that the behavior may appear to originate from a trusted application, but it does not provide evidence that any named organization or individual performed it.\"},{\"summary\":\"Image Spoofing and Notepad.exe provide a benign process-image facade\",\"explanation\":\"Image Spoofing is explicitly linked to Notepad.exe as the benign executable recorded by endpoint detection and response tooling, and Process Hollowing is separately described as starting Notepad.exe in a suspended state before overwriting its memory [Data: Entities (473, 472, 475); Relationships (580, 583)]. Process Image Manipulation is identified as another name for Image Spoofing, showing that these records represent closely related terminology rather than unrelated capabilities [Data: Entities (474); Relationships (579)].\\n\\nThe significance of this relationship is that a familiar executable image can serve as the apparent identity of a process while the process contents have been altered. The supplied evidence supports a concern about identity deception and detection ambiguity, not a conclusion that Notepad.exe itself is malicious.\"},{\"summary\":\"Module Stomping extends injection into legitimate loaded modules\",\"explanation\":\"Module Stomping is described as an in-memory DLL-based injection technique in which a legitimate, unused DLL is loaded into a remote process and its executable section is overwritten or repurposed to host shellcode; a new thread then begins execution at the modified code [Data: Entities (185); Relationships (176)]. CreateRemoteThread is identified as a possible execution mechanism for process-injection or module-stomping activity [Data: Relationships (176)].\\n\\nThe community also connects Module Stomping to Black Hills Information Security and F-Secure through cited articles concerning DLL jumping and hiding malicious code with module stomping [Data: Entities (506, 507); Relationships (639, 640)]. These relationships establish research or publication associations, but do not establish that either organization developed, endorsed, or used the technique.\"},{\"summary\":\"Process Doppelgänging uses transactional file behavior to reduce on-disk traces\",\"explanation\":\"Process Doppelgänging abuses Transactional NTFS by modifying a legitimate file within a transaction, launching a process from the altered version, and then rolling back the transaction so the original file is restored [Data: Entities (476, 480, 491); Relationships (586, 609)]. The technique is therefore connected to both transactional file operations and rollback behavior rather than relying solely on process-memory replacement.\\n\\nTelemetry-based manipulation detection is linked to Process Doppelgänging because endpoint telemetry can identify transactional file operations and related process-creation behavior [Data: Entities (503); Relationships (631)]. This indicates a detection path for the technique, while also showing that the community includes both offensive-evasion concepts and defensive monitoring concepts.\"},{\"summary\":\"Detection methods target discrepancies between process memory, files, and telemetry\",\"explanation\":\"Process Memory/File Comparison is described as comparing the memory contents of a running process with the contents of its executable file; the relationship to Image Spoofing states that this can reveal a supposedly legitimate process image that has been modified or replaced [Data: Entities (502, 473); Relationships (629)]. Telemetry-Based Manipulation Detection is separately associated with identifying process or memory manipulations linked to image spoofing and code injection [Data: Entities (503); Relationships (631)].\\n\\nThese defensive entities address different observable signals: static-versus-memory discrepancies and behavioral telemetry. The data notes that memory/file comparison can be performance intensive [Data: Entities (502)], indicating a potential operational tradeoff, but it does not quantify detection effectiveness or deployment scope.\"},{\"summary\":\"The community contains multiple related file and memory-manipulation primitives\",\"explanation\":\"Process Herpaderping connects malicious executable content with benign executable content, reflecting rapid replacement intended to affect what scanners observe [Data: Entities (492, 493); Relationships (611)]. Process Ghosting connects an empty file to a semi-deleted file and then to malicious executable content, representing a file-state sequence used to complicate inspection [Data: Entities (494, 495, 492); Relationships (613, 615)]. Process Hollowing connects a suspended process to process-memory overwrite and malicious executable content [Data: Entities (488, 489, 492); Relationships (604, 606)].\\n\\nThese relationships show that the community is not a single technique but a family of methods that manipulate either executable files, process memory, or both. The shared technical objective is concealment of malicious content from ordinary inspection, although the supplied records do not provide prevalence, exploitability, or incident frequency.\"}],\"rating\":8.0,\"rating_explanation\":\"The community merits a high impact rating because it consolidates several sophisticated process-injection and defense-evasion techniques that can undermine endpoint inspection, while the data does not establish a specific actor, victim, or confirmed deployment.\"}", "refusal": null, "role": "assistant", "annotations": [], "audio": null, "function_call": null, "tool_calls": null, "provider_specific_fields": {"refusal": null}}, "provider_specific_fields": {}}], "created": 1788086475, "model": "gpt-5.6-luna", "object": "chat.completion", "moderation": null, "service_tier": "default", "system_fingerprint": null, "usage": {"completion_tokens": 1591, "prompt_tokens": 4169, "total_tokens": 5760, "completion_tokens_details": {"accepted_prediction_tokens": 0, "audio_tokens": 0, "reasoning_tokens": 55, "rejected_prediction_tokens": 0}, "prompt_tokens_details": {"audio_tokens": 0, "cache_write_tokens": 4166, "cached_tokens": 0}}, "formatted_response": null, "content": "{\"title\":\"Process Injection and Image-Evasion Techniques\",\"summary\":\"This community centers on Windows process-injection and executable-image evasion techniques, including Process Hollowing, Process Herpaderping, Process Ghosting, Process Doppelgänging, Image Spoofing, and Module Stomping. These techniques are connected through their use of manipulated process memory, deceptive executable content, file-system behavior, or legitimate-looking process images to conceal malicious execution. Supporting technical entities include CreateProcess, WriteProcessMemory(), suspended processes, process resumption, Transactional NTFS, and Notepad.exe as a benign-looking target. The community also includes detection approaches—process memory/file comparison and telemetry-ba